      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michael Berry", "Yakir Aharonov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase revealed that quantum phases have observable consequences and geometric structure, shaping broad areas of physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's work established the physical significance of potentials in the Aharonov-Bohm effect; Berry identified the geometric phase in adiabatic evolution. Their contributions are distinct and both central.",
        "maturity": "The effects have been confirmed and applied across many decades of experiment and theory.",
        "uncertainties": ["The ballot bundles two related but historically separate discoveries, so a single prize citation would need a clear scope."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B013",
      "proposed_laureates": ["A. Peter Young", "Bertrand I. Halperin", "David R. Nelson"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The defect-mediated theory of two-dimensional melting and its hexatic phase are a deep, general account of how order changes in low-dimensional matter.",
        "attribution": "The KTHNY framework emerged from closely connected work by Halperin, Nelson, and Young; crediting all three is well supported by the formulation represented here.",
        "maturity": "The theory is established and has substantial experimental support, with lasting influence in condensed matter and soft matter physics.",
        "uncertainties": ["The extent to which the theory describes every real two-dimensional melting system varies with material and conditions."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Antoine Georges", "Dieter Vollhardt", "Gabriel Kotliar"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory gave strongly correlated electron physics a powerful framework that connects local many-body physics to material behavior.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Kotliar, and Vollhardt were central to developing and establishing the framework and its applications.",
        "maturity": "It has been extensively developed and used for decades, with continuing relevance to correlated materials.",
        "uncertainties": ["The framework's local approximation is highly useful but does not capture all spatial correlations, which limits some applications."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B049",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexei Kitaev", "Andrew M. Steane", "Peter W. Shor"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction and fault tolerance established that quantum computation can in principle overcome noise, a foundational result in quantum information physics.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane independently introduced influential error-correcting codes, while Kitaev developed topological codes and a broader fault-tolerant framework.",
        "maturity": "The theory is mature and has guided experimental progress, though large-scale fault-tolerant computation remains an ongoing engineering challenge.",
        "uncertainties": ["The ballot combines several major strands of work, and the balance of credit among them depends on how narrowly the prize is defined."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Juan Ignacio Cirac", "Peter Zoller", "Rainer Blatt"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped ions made quantum control and computation experimentally concrete, creating a highly precise platform for quantum simulation and information processing.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller supplied foundational theoretical proposals; Blatt led landmark experimental demonstrations. The theory and experimental realization are complementary.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been developed and validated over decades, with increasingly capable experiments.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field includes important contributions beyond these three, and the division between foundational proposal and later platform development needs careful delimitation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Claudio Castelnovo", "Roderich Moessner", "Steven T. Bramwell"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spin ice revealed emergent magnetic monopole-like excitations in real materials, a striking demonstration of fractionalized behavior in a frustrated magnet.",
        "attribution": "Castelnovo and Moessner developed the monopole interpretation with Bramwell, whose experimental work helped establish its physical significance.",
        "maturity": "The phenomena have been investigated for many years and are well established, though the monopoles are emergent excitations rather than fundamental particles.",
        "uncertainties": ["The precise scope of the monopole claim should be stated carefully because these excitations are effective, material-dependent quasiparticles."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B035",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have pushed precision measurement to an extraordinary level and opened new tests of fundamental physics and applications in geodesy and timekeeping.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently developed leading approaches and made decisive experimental advances.",
        "maturity": "The technology is experimentally mature, with repeated precision improvements and broad international validation.",
        "uncertainties": ["The continuing rapid improvement makes it difficult to draw a fixed boundary around the decisive achievement."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B032",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Marc Mézard", "Riccardo Zecchina"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The cavity method and message-passing theory exposed glassy structure in hard computational problems and showed how statistical physics can yield powerful general methods.",
        "attribution": "Mézard and Zecchina made central contributions to applying cavity ideas to random constraint-satisfaction problems and deriving algorithmic consequences.",
        "maturity": "The framework has had lasting influence in statistical physics, computer science, and inference, with a substantial body of follow-up work.",
        "uncertainties": ["Its Nobel case depends on how broadly the committee values foundational theoretical bridges to computation relative to discoveries in physical systems."]
      }
    }
  ]
